# Updating CoreX in {{ name }}

This repository carries a copy of the CoreX framework beside the {{ name }} site, and takes each
framework release by merging it. This is the checklist for doing that. The full procedure, with
the reasoning behind each step, is in the framework's documentation:
`docs/en/05-deployment/updating-a-client-site.md`.

**The rule that keeps an update conflict-free:** client work never edits a framework-owned path.
Everything of {{ name }}'s lives in `sites/{{ site_dir }}/` and in
`.github/workflows/site-{{ site_dir }}.yml`. Every other path in this repository is the
framework's, the root included.

## Before

- [ ] `git status` is clean, and you are on a new branch such as `chore/corex-<version>-update`.
- [ ] `npm run verify:framework` passes. If it reports `DRIFT`, a framework file was edited here.
      Resolve that first, or the merge will conflict on it.
- [ ] Read the **Client impact** section of `CHANGELOG.md` for every release between the one
      recorded in `sites/{{ site_dir }}/corex-baseline.json` and the one you are taking.
- [ ] Note how the public pages look and behave now, to compare afterwards.

## Update

- [ ] `git fetch upstream --tags`
- [ ] `git merge <release tag>` — for example `git merge v1.2.3`. A conflict in a framework-owned
      path means a local edit: take the framework's version unless it is a recorded exception.
- [ ] `composer install` and `npm ci`
- [ ] `npm run build` — the framework's own assets.
- [ ] Build the client's. In `sites/{{ site_dir }}/{{ plugin_slug }}` and
      `sites/{{ site_dir }}/{{ theme_slug }}`, wherever there is a `package.json`, run `npm ci`
      (or `npm install` where there is no lockfile) and then `npm run build`.
- [ ] `wp corex migrate` — applies any database schema change the release carries. Run it again in
      each environment as the release reaches it.

## Verify

- [ ] `npm run verify:framework -- --record <release tag>` — records the new baseline.
- [ ] `npm run verify:framework` — must pass. It now compares against the release you took, so a
      pass means the framework files here are exactly that release's.
- [ ] `composer test` and `npm run test:js` — the framework's suites.
- [ ] The {{ name }} site's own tests.
- [ ] Compare the public pages with what you noted before.
- [ ] Commit, including `sites/{{ site_dir }}/corex-baseline.json`, and open a pull request.

## If a framework defect blocks {{ name }}

Report it in the framework's repository and take the fix by update. If {{ name }} cannot wait,
patch the file here and list it under `exceptions` in `corex-baseline.json`, with a `reason` and
the `upstream` issue. `npm run verify:framework` reports the exception on every run, and fails it
as `STALE` once the framework has the fix. Delete the exception then; do not re-apply the patch.
